WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.386 --> 00:00:09.010
JEFF JOHNSON: Welcome
back, developers.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:09.790
Jeff here.

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:12.330
We spent a lot of time
talking about Cloud Foundry,

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:15.310
but today I'm joined by Sharat
of the Stackdriver team,

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.650
who promises to show us
how to debug our production

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.485
applications with
Google Stackdriver.

00:00:19.485 --> 00:00:20.610
SHARAT SHROFF: Howdy, Jeff.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:21.880
JEFF JOHNSON: Howdy, Sharat.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.589
Can you tell me a bit
about Stackdriver?

00:00:23.589 --> 00:00:24.630
SHARAT SHROFF: Of course.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:28.510
So Stackdriver is a suite
of logging, monitoring,

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:30.880
and other diagnostic
tools, which is primarily

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.880
targeted towards developers
who are building and running

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.400
their application in the cloud.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.350
It is designed to alert
developers of problems

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.700
occurring in production
and easily diagnose

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.425
the root cause of the problems.

00:00:44.425 --> 00:00:46.400
JEFF JOHNSON: All
right, sounds great.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.910
SHARAT SHROFF: So one good
way to experience Stackdriver

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:50.200
is by actually using the tool.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.380
So why don't I just give a quick
whirlwind demo of Stackdriver

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:54.530
diagnostic tools?

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:55.430
JEFF JOHNSON: All
right, let's dive in.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:56.500
SHARAT SHROFF: All right.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.750
So let me open up
an application here

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.930
that I've got running on
the Google Cloud Platform,

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:06.590
and let's start with logging.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:12.040
So every developer, of course,
spends time looking at logs.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.416
And what you would see
here is a fairly simple

00:01:16.416 --> 00:01:17.540
straightforward log viewer.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.870
And this log viewer has--

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.850
of course you can
view logs as they get

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.740
generated by the application.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.360
But we have added
a few more things

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.200
like the ability to filter
by label or do text search.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.600
And we can do things
like log base metrics.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.852
We can export these
logs to BigQuery.

00:01:33.852 --> 00:01:36.310
And then you have the whole
power of BigQuery-- the ability

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:38.143
to slice and dice and
[? permute ?] the data

00:01:38.143 --> 00:01:39.910
in any which you
want, available.

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:41.800
And all of this-- the
export to BigQuery--

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.080
can happen in just
a couple of clicks.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.620
But one thing that
we observed was

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:51.730
while developers and engineers
take comfort in the fact

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.340
that there is data
in logs, they really

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.600
don't like wallowing in it.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:56.433
JEFF JOHNSON: Right.

00:01:56.433 --> 00:01:58.640
SHARAT SHROFF: They don't
like spending time like--

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.777
you could easily spend
days looking at the logs,

00:02:00.777 --> 00:02:02.610
trying to find that one
specification that's

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.480
occurring in production.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.220
So with that in
mind, what we did was

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:10.360
we said we can probably do a
better job of pre-processing

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.010
these logs as they come in.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.950
And so we created
Error Reporting.

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.920
And the best way to
think of Error Reporting

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.040
is it's a snapshot.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.930
It's a single
snapshot of everything

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:26.140
that's happening in your
application represented

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:27.430
on one page.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:29.470
We take all the logs
that's generated,

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.260
scan through each of
them in near real time,

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:34.570
and then we
intelligently group these

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:37.450
into different error buckets.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:39.190
And for each of
these error buckets

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:41.840
we provide some information
like how often it's occurring,

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.740
a quick histogram of how
frequently it occurs,

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:50.114
and how severe the issue is.

00:02:50.114 --> 00:02:52.030
When it was first seen,
when it was last seen.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.400
We have the ability
to slice these error

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.900
groups by time or by versions
and by the application itself.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.770
And you can of course
click on this auto-reload

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.020
that allows you to
effectively keep

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.000
getting the stream
of information

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.660
be updated in near real time.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:11.580
JEFF JOHNSON: Wow.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.455
SHARAT SHROFF: You can
click on any of this--

00:03:13.455 --> 00:03:16.810
let's go ahead and click on
one of these error groups here.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.440
And what you see here,
as you would expect,

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.880
is every single
instance of that error

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.360
group captured in this view.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:28.080
So you can drill into any
of those instances of errors

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.850
within this error group.

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:32.250
In addition to, of course,
showing you the occurrences

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:34.830
as we described and when
it was first and last seen,

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:36.960
we give you a more
detailed histogram

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.220
of where this is occurring.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.156
And we capture the stack trace.

00:03:40.156 --> 00:03:43.230
The view the most
engineers are used to

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:48.660
is to basically look
at this mind numbing

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:49.759
view of this stack trace.

00:03:49.759 --> 00:03:50.550
JEFF JOHNSON: Yeah.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:54.810
SHARAT SHROFF: My eyes glaze
over whenever I look at this.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.960
So what we try to do is,
we scan through every one

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:01.950
of these stack traces
and we try to surface

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.690
what we believe is the
most relevant portion

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:05.790
of the entire stack trace.

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:07.248
You can always view
the whole thing

00:04:07.248 --> 00:04:09.840
by clicking on the Show
All, but the past view

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.650
is meant to represent
what we believe

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.150
is the most relevant section
where we believe the problem is

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:17.970
occurring, where we
believe is the exact source

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:19.230
of the problem.

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:25.460
So with all of this,
what you have is

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:28.020
developers can get a
snapshot very quickly,

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:30.210
drill into the snapshot
of the specific instance

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:34.110
where the error is occurring,
and have some clues on where

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:36.880
the exact source of that error.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:39.740
So if you left it at that,
you can imagine a developer

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:43.280
coming to the error reporting
page, going to the logs,

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:45.570
and trying to understand
the root cause.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.300
So the common next
steps we observed

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:49.520
is that developers
would then try

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:51.290
to reproduce that
problem that's occurring

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:55.430
in production on a separate dev
box, on a separate test box.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:58.460
And then they would go and
insert some breakpoints,

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:00.770
and then inspect the
application state

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:02.780
to try to understand
where the problem could

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:04.700
be exactly occurring,
trying to find

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:06.164
the root cause of the problem.

00:05:06.164 --> 00:05:07.580
When they believed
that they found

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:10.370
the root cause, what
developers would tend to do

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:14.930
is to write some more code and
insert some logs and then try

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:21.000
to make sure they validated that
same hypothesis in production.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.500
And so that would require the
developer to write some code,

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:27.432
redeploy the entire application,
wait for the problem to occur,

00:05:27.432 --> 00:05:29.390
and then bingo, you would
see the logs and say,

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:30.931
now I'm sure that
I've found the root

00:05:30.931 --> 00:05:33.286
cause, let me go write the
lines to go fix the problem.

00:05:33.286 --> 00:05:35.160
JEFF JOHNSON: Yeah, a
few hours later, right?

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:36.150
SHARAT SHROFF: It's
a few hours later.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.010
Some places take
a few days later--

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:38.360
JEFF JOHNSON: Sure.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:40.151
SHARAT SHROFF: --because
the problems might

00:05:40.151 --> 00:05:42.100
occur at different places.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.060
So especially you
think of scenarios

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:47.060
where developers have rolled
out only one code to only one

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:49.490
section of the
instances and not all

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:53.030
because it might have some
impact to the application

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:54.950
running in production itself.

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:58.250
So what we did was we
started asking ourselves

00:05:58.250 --> 00:05:59.720
some basic questions.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:03.440
Could we capture the
application state in production

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:06.950
when the application was running
without actually impeding

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:09.470
the application itself, without
pausing the application,

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:11.690
without impacting the
customers or the users

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:13.460
of the application?

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:15.440
We asked ourselves the
question whether we

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:19.040
can insert lock
statements on the fly

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:21.680
when the application is
running without, again, having

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:25.580
to write any lines of code.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:30.630
So keeping that in mind,
we built the debugger,

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:33.280
the Stackdriver
debugger experience.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.780
The best way to think of the
Stackdriver debugger experience

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:38.630
is, let's take one of
the specific instance

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:39.620
of applications here.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:45.080
Let's look at this.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:47.050
We've got what we
have highlighted here,

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:50.300
and the relevant and the
past few of stack trace,

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:54.010
is a specific file
name and a line number.

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.970
And that's highlighted to you
as a link, as a handy link.

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:59.630
If you click on that, we
pop open the debugger.

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:02.240
And what is happening
right now, the debugger

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:05.930
is trying to take a
snapshot of that application

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:07.260
as it's running in real time.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.660
And I think, as
we were speaking,

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:11.470
it already took this snapshot.

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:16.400
And specifically
what happens here

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:21.860
is no matter where the next
time this code is executed--

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:26.650
I think this application
has multiple instances.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:29.450
It's running on
multiple instances.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:31.180
It doesn't matter
when this occurs next,

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:34.010
it's going to basically
capture the snapshot

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:35.990
and bring it all back
into this debug view.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:36.740
JEFF JOHNSON: Wow.

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:37.910
So you don't even
have to pick, this

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:39.830
is my special instance
I want to debug--

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:40.160
SHARAT SHROFF: That's right.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:40.950
JEFF JOHNSON: It's my
entire application.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:42.783
SHARAT SHROFF: It's
your entire application.

00:07:42.783 --> 00:07:44.240
It can run on one instance.

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:46.677
It can run on 100,000
different instances.

00:07:46.677 --> 00:07:47.760
There's no problem at all.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:49.093
JEFF JOHNSON: That's incredible.

00:07:49.093 --> 00:07:53.660
SHARAT SHROFF: We will scale
completely without any problem.

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:57.320
And you can also add things
like regular expressions,

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.220
which have to evaluate to true.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:03.410
So you can imagine
taking a snapshot

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:05.490
when a specific
condition occurs,

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:08.060
or when a specific
expression evaluates

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:10.610
to true, because you don't
want to be taking snapshots

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:12.140
for every time that
the code occurs.

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:14.510
It's when the code occurs
and the user equals Jeff,

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:15.610
you can totally do that.

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:16.610
JEFF JOHNSON: Wow.

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:18.470
SHARAT SHROFF: And
when you do that,

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:21.350
you instantly are able to
capture the application state.

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:22.730
JEFF JOHNSON: Wow.

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:26.990
SHARAT SHROFF: We can
also go insert logs

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:32.206
into the application
logs by a simple command.

00:08:32.206 --> 00:08:34.039
And you can do that
through the Cloud Shell.

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:37.520
So you basically click on the
Cloud Shell as you're used to,

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:40.730
and then we have very
simple debug commands

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:42.320
handy that you can use.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:45.650
And you can just go ahead
and insert logs there.

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:48.920
One thing that we also noticed
was that developers really

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:51.740
like to look at their
logs in conjunction

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:52.700
with their source code.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:54.710
So not only can you
navigate your source code,

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:56.900
which is automatically
selected for you--

00:08:56.900 --> 00:09:01.030
the right version
number and file names

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:03.120
are automatically
selected for you--

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:05.584
but we also show you the
relevant logs in this.

00:09:05.584 --> 00:09:08.000
And you can use the same things
like filtering that you're

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.290
used to in the previous few.

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:14.080
You can also look at
the various snapshots

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:17.680
that I've taken
before, or you can

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:22.660
see the snapshots that have
been taken by other users trying

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:24.700
to debug this application.

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:27.550
With everything that we
have done in Stackdriver,

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:31.060
you can share the same view.

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:35.140
We enabled deep linking
into our experience.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:37.570
It's as simple as copying
and pasting this URL.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:40.220
I could just copy this URL
and send it over to you.

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:42.610
And when you open it, you
would see the exact same view,

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:46.090
with the exact same highlights,
with the same snapshot summary.

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:49.960
So that when we decide to
debug an application together,

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:52.270
it's as simple as just
copy pasting this URL.

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:52.990
JEFF JOHNSON: And
that's something

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:54.640
that I traditionally
have to say, OK,

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.190
come on over to my box.

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.000
I've got it just right.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.770
Hope I don't hit
refresh or something.

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:00.280
SHARAT SHROFF: That's right.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:01.210
JEFF JOHNSON: That's incredible.

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:01.780
SHARAT SHROFF: It's pretty cool.

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:02.770
We like it.

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:05.080
JEFF JOHNSON: Well, seeing
all these amazing features,

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:07.300
I would love to get these
integrated with Cloud Foundry.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:08.110
SHARAT SHROFF: Let's do it.

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:09.693
JEFF JOHNSON: Let's
jump right into it

00:10:09.693 --> 00:10:12.100
and see how we can
get this thing set up.

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:14.620
So I've got a GO
application, this

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:17.170
is running in Cloud
Foundry as I said.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:19.630
Can you show me how I can get
it set up with the debugger

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:20.620
and error reporting?

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.578
SHARAT SHROFF: So one of
the first things to do

00:10:22.578 --> 00:10:24.610
is we first bring the--

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:26.759
your application is
written in GO, correct?

00:10:26.759 --> 00:10:27.550
JEFF JOHONSON: Mhm.

00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:29.175
SHARAT SHROFF: So
what we'll want to do

00:10:29.175 --> 00:10:31.780
is bring in the
debugger agent for GO.

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:33.280
For that you'll
need to modify how

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:35.710
your app starts, to
run this agent instead

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:39.130
of running your app directly.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:43.420
So at this point
maybe we should do it.

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:45.690
JEFF JOHNSON: All right.

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:46.420
All right.

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:47.920
So I'm going to
pull in the debugger

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:50.610
agent to the same
folder my projects in,

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:53.190
and I'll modify my PROC
file, which specifies

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:54.387
how my application starts.

00:10:54.387 --> 00:10:56.220
Instead of running the
application directly,

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.807
I will just run that
debugger agent instead.

00:10:58.807 --> 00:10:59.640
SHARAT SHROFF: Yeah.

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:01.140
And next what
you'll need to do is

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:04.170
provide some kind of source
context for the debugger,

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:08.280
so that it knows which specific
application version it's

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:09.390
working with.

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:12.000
And this can be generated
with a simple GClock command.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:13.460
JEFF JOHNSON: OK.

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:14.880
That sounds like
an excellent job

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:17.167
for a continuous integration,
continuous delivery

00:11:17.167 --> 00:11:18.000
sort of environment.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:18.870
SHARAT SHROFF: Exactly.

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:20.661
JEFF JOHNSON: I'll go
ahead and just put it

00:11:20.661 --> 00:11:22.940
in my deploy script for now.

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:25.289
So how does
permissioning work here?

00:11:25.289 --> 00:11:27.455
SHARAT SHROFF: So if you're
running your application

00:11:27.455 --> 00:11:32.390
in GCP, the debugger will
pick up all the service

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:35.150
account credentials
for the virtual machine

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:36.190
it's running on.

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:38.150
But if you're not
running on GCP,

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:40.760
you can provide credentials
to the service account

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:42.590
through a simple
environment variable.

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:44.840
JEFF JOHNSON: Well, my Cloud
Foundry is running on GCP

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:47.480
so I guess I'll get all those
service account credentials

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:48.740
automatically.

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:51.391
But for folks who are running
on other cloud providers,

00:11:51.391 --> 00:11:53.390
we'll put information in
the example application

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:55.445
on how to set up credentials.

00:11:55.445 --> 00:11:56.320
SHARAT SHROFF: Great.

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:00.380
So now for the integration part,
we'll pull in error reporting.

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:03.640
For the GO version you'll
want to place error.catch

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:06.770
in a defer statement whenever
you want to catch and report

00:12:06.770 --> 00:12:07.580
panics.

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:08.630
JEFF JOHNSON: OK.

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:10.940
So I'm going to pull in
the SDK with GO vendor.

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:20.060
And then hop into my
program and import the SDK.

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:21.750
I'm just going to
set up a variable

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:23.136
to hang onto the error's client.

00:12:26.798 --> 00:12:31.090
OK, and just initialize that.

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:33.340
So my application is
running with an HTTP

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:34.810
stack that supports middleware.

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:38.039
So I'm just going to paste in
an empty piece of middleware.

00:12:38.039 --> 00:12:39.830
And in there is where
I'm going to put that

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:43.220
defer errors.catch statement.

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:46.090
So if an error occurs
higher up in my HTTP stack,

00:12:46.090 --> 00:12:48.640
in the Handler, it will
get caught at this point

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:49.410
and reported out.

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:55.990
So with that in place,
all I need to do

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:57.701
is install my middleware.

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:02.895
And that's it?

00:13:02.895 --> 00:13:04.690
SHARAT SHROFF: That's it.

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:07.050
So now we can view the
errors that are generated

00:13:07.050 --> 00:13:09.120
and you can start
debugging in production.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:09.900
